WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
So yesterday, I interviewed Ben Dixon, host of&nbsp;
the Benjamin Dixon Show, and we talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:14.000
what could Joe Biden focus on next early last&nbsp;
week? The rumor was the report was that tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.720
reform would be next after covid relief now being&nbsp;
done. Earlier this week, we heard it's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:28.000
a three trillion dollar infrastructure, schools&nbsp;
and families package, which will be next. But in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.680
the back of all of this is the push to expand&nbsp;
voting rights. And there's a, I would argue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.880
a very strong case to be made that the advantage&nbsp;
of going next to voting rights is twofold. Number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.680
one, it's of paramount importance to win elections&nbsp;
that as many people be able to vote as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.720
and that Republicans not be allowed&nbsp;
to succeed with what they are doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:01:00.800
Remember that Republicans, in the aftermath of the&nbsp;
2020 election launched proposed roads are starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.600
to work on more than two hundred and fifty&nbsp;
different voter suppression bills across more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:13.040
40 states. So not only is the issue of expanding&nbsp;
voting rights relevant for winning elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.480
it's a particularly timely importance because&nbsp;
Republicans are trying to do the exact opposite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.120
In addition to this, there's the context of the&nbsp;
filibuster. Now, we'll get in a moment to what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.480
in the voting rights bill that the House passed.&nbsp;
But if you were thinking, as Democrats are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.600
we may need to do something with the filibuster&nbsp;
to get things done in the next two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.040
with Democrats having the White House, House&nbsp;
and Senate. And it's the filibuster being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.440
there that may prevent us from doing that.&nbsp;
Is there any better issue over which we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:51.600
justify doing? Again, it's a modification of&nbsp;
the filibuster elimination. We don't know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:59.280
But what would be a better case to make than&nbsp;
this is about voting? Republicans are blocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.200
voting rights. We must do something with&nbsp;
the filibuster. So that's the if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:12.800
to make a case for why it should indeed be voting&nbsp;
rights expansion that should be focused on first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.600
you could make the case that it's because&nbsp;
Republicans are actively doing the opposite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.800
So Democrats must push back against those two&nbsp;
hundred and fifty bills, because if you believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:29.200
you'll need filibuster changes to get other things&nbsp;
done, it's probably politically more viable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.320
To justify changing the filibuster over something&nbsp;
as important as like, listen, you know, with guns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.240
I think it would be justified in theory to&nbsp;
modify the filibuster, to try to get things done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.880
But I think from a public relations standpoint,&nbsp;
it would be more difficult with something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:54.480
this is voting rights. You arguably have the&nbsp;
most justifiable reason to do it. Now, would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:01.200
there be any Republican support for this voting&nbsp;
rights bill in the Senate? My understanding is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:08.160
and again, this is a topic that often there's&nbsp;
disagreement about. I don't believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.160
there's a budget reconciliation argument to&nbsp;
be made for voting rights because it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.680
it's not a budget issue. It's not a spending&nbsp;
issue, although certainly some of the initiatives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.800
cost money. Maybe I'm wrong and you could do&nbsp;
voting rights via budget reconciliation. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:30.480
not read anything so far that says you could. And&nbsp;
in the House, the 800 page bill passed basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:36.160
along party lines. And it does a ton of stuff, not&nbsp;
all of which Republicans want, much of which some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:43.200
Republicans want. None of this bill would create&nbsp;
minimum standards for states in terms of how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:50.320
must run elections, federal standards that states&nbsp;
must abide by. It would basically destroy a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:57.200
the voter ID proposals and laws in a variety&nbsp;
of different states. It would restore voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:05.600
rights to former felons, period, not states will&nbsp;
vote as as a referendum. No, it just it restores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:11.840
voting rights to former felons. It puts in place&nbsp;
requirements like automatic voter registration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.880
no excuse mail in balloting, which means&nbsp;
you don't need a qualifying reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:25.760
explain why you want to vote by mail. You can just&nbsp;
say, I want to vote by mail and that's it. And a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.200
lot of these provisions are the exact opposite of&nbsp;
what Republicans at the state level are doing now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.080
It's important to remember many of the Republicans&nbsp;
in the Senate who would be voting on this bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.240
don't necessarily have the same&nbsp;
perspective or opinion on these issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:47.200
as the state level Republican legislators that are&nbsp;
working on the and on the voter suppression bills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.320
But still, I don't know that you get any&nbsp;
Republican support for this. The other thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.080
is on gerrymandering and gerrymandering, we've&nbsp;
talked about many times over the years this bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.400
that has passed the House and we'll see what&nbsp;
happens in the Senate would require states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.520
to use independent commissions to draw&nbsp;
congressional districts. And right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.000
this would significantly hurt Republicans.&nbsp;
You could imagine a situation in the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.360
where it's Democrats who have gotten&nbsp;
to draw the majority of the districts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.560
and such a provision could hurt Democrats. But&nbsp;
remember that even who gets to draw the districts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:30.560
aside because of the urban rural divide, it&nbsp;
favors Republicans in in terms of districts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.120
drawing at at a sort of core level. So that's&nbsp;
a huge deal. Republicans know that part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.640
reason they win some districts in purple states,&nbsp;
even in red states, is by drawing them in very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:47.680
particular ways if we've exposed before. So this&nbsp;
is a this is a major deal if it could be done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.080
And what needs to be balanced by Joe Biden and&nbsp;
by Democrats here in terms of determining whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.360
this is the next thing that they want to deal&nbsp;
with is, number one, the importance and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:04.560
urgency given that there's an election now in,&nbsp;
what, two, five minus 19 months were 19 months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.760
from the midterms. And if Republicans just do what&nbsp;
they're doing in all of these different states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.040
it will hurt the left in twenty twenty two,&nbsp;
there's no doubt. So there's that urgency. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:23.520
the justification for using enfranchisement as a&nbsp;
valid reason to do something with the filibuster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.280
maybe do away with maybe just reform and mandate&nbsp;
attacking a mandate, a talking filibuster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:37.040
maybe make some other changes. And because it&nbsp;
could set up the possibility of the infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.480
plan and of other things in the remainder of&nbsp;
these first two years of Joe Biden's term. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.360
that's the gist of it. I'm curious to hear from&nbsp;
you. What do you want Joe Biden and Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.320
focusing on next? Should it be the tax reform&nbsp;
plan, which I think politically is the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.600
difficult at this point in time, given the&nbsp;
circumstances with the pandemic and economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:03.200
which we'll talk about in a moment? Should it&nbsp;
be the infrastructure spending plan where there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:09.840
is maybe some more Republican support, but they&nbsp;
will be opposed to much of it? Or is it the voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.480
rights expansion? Let me know what you would&nbsp;
like to see Joe Biden and Democrats tackle next.

